# Features
## **Server**
### **Shared Broadcast**
Rather than having multiple messages sent to each client, the server utilizes a "shared" input that is fowarded to each client
### **Message forwarding**
Each message is forwarded to each client, regardless of tags. Clients handle tags.
### **Moderation**
Before forwarding, the server can moderate messages. Instead of the message, it will simply send a notice stating the message has been modified.

## **Client**
### **Sync Tcp, Async broadcast**
The main() function spawns a blocking tcp stream task, and uses an async broadcaster task to listen for forwaded messages
### **Broadcast**
There are tags at the top of the broadcast message, if the user holds the tag the forwarded messages are ignored
### **Chat Logs**
The chat history is displayed on the command line

## **Shared**
### **Channels**
Each channel contains:
One server, as many clients as the server can handle
### **Channel surfing**
If the server/client likes, they can move between channels.
### **Authentication**
Clients must auth with servers at the beginning in order for the channels to work.
### **Encryption**
Messages are encrypted and decrypted.

## **Process Structure**
Connection/Message via TCP from client -> Recieved, broadcast subscribed to client -> Response formulated, sent to all clients -> 
                                                                                                        |
                                                                                                        |
                                                                                                        > If a message is recieved at this time, input will be saved and restored
Once updated, the chat logs/terminal are flushed and updated with any lagged messages -> Repeats until connections are closed
